  "name": "French musette (Parisian accordion-led)",
  "family": "global",
  "lineage": "French musette accordion-led popular-folk genre developed in early-twentieth-century Paris from the convergence of Auvergnat-immigrant cabrette-bagpipe dance-music with Italian-immigrant accordion-playing in the working-class Parisian guinguette dance-hall and bistro scenes around Bastille and the rue de Lappe. Characterized by waltz-and-java rhythmic base, virtuoso accordion-melodic lead, and the canonical valse musette dance-floor accompaniment. The interwar 1920s-1930s period at Bal Tabarin and Balajo dance-halls anchored institutional crystallization. Distinguished from later French chanson by musette's instrumental-dance (vs literary-lyrical) primary functional orientation.",
